{"chunk_id": "f6f290befd68", "text": "Yudhishthira, believing fate cannot be avoided and bound by the king\u2019s command and the warrior code, agrees to another game of dice despite knowing it will destroy him. The narrator compares this fatal lapse to Rama being deceived by the golden deer\u2014an omen that minds cloud when calamity approaches. Sakuni proposes a final stake: the losing side must live twelve years in the forest and a thirteenth year incognito, or repeat the exile if discovered. Ignoring warnings from those present, Yudhishthira accepts. Sakuni immediately declares victory, condemning the Pandavas to exile. As they discard their royal garments and don deer\u2011skins, Dussasana mocks them cruelly, boasting of Duryodhana\u2019s triumph and humiliating Draupadi. This moment seals the Pandavas\u2019 exile and intensifies the moral contrast between the two sides.\nYudhishthira accepts a second dice game staking exile; Sakuni wins immediately, sending the Pandavas into forest exile as Dussasana mocks them.\nThemes: fate vs free will, gambling and deception, humiliation of the Pandavas, exile vow, Kuru court cruelty", "vector": [-0.007018096279352903, -0.017612382769584656, -0.012226895429193974, 0.016453301534056664, -0.03197368234395981, 0.010134894400835037, -0.04285774007439613, -0.01329409796744585, -0.014007922261953354, -0.024665817618370056, 0.027620060369372368, 0.005434961058199406, 0.025499790906906128, 0.017852680757641792, -0.0003666301490738988, 0.007710718084126711, 0.03969147056341171, 0.002724548103287816, 0.04234887659549713, -0.016000976786017418, -0.007717785891145468, -0.012587341479957104, -0.007442150730639696, 0.003756413236260414, -0.020340465009212494, 0.028369223698973656, 0.01180284097790718, -0.009392799809575081, 0.007117042317986488, -0.01181697566062212, 0.0021361729595810175, -0.005880217533558607, 0.01576068066060543, -0.015195273794233799, -0.035450927913188934, 0.010763907805085182, -0.0034913793206214905, -0.0062901368